From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 22:12:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(model): a park refused for the host's memory floor says
 what it read

A park that would leave the host under park_host_floor_bytes is refused
(412 host_memory_floor) with four figures: what was available, what other
parks in flight are copying, the bytes to park, the floor. Nothing was
logged, and "available" is itself a decision - the host's MemAvailable or a
memory cgroup's room, whichever is smaller - so a refusal could not say
WHICH limited it. A refusal seen during a gate (six parks of 605 MB, then
412 while several models were starting) could not be told from a full
container or a host whose page cache was not yet counted.

The host is now read once into xsysinfo.MemoryReading - MemAvailable, each
memory cgroup's limit, working set and room, and the ZFS ARC's size and its
floor where the host has one (/proc/spl/kstat/zfs/arcstats; Linux's
MemAvailable does not count the ARC, which gives memory back under
pressure) - and the park is decided on that very reading, by the same rule
as before (AllocatableMemory is ReadMemory().Allocatable(); the ARC is said
and decided on by nobody). A refusal logs a warning with every figure, and
its answer ends with the reading:

  ... a floor of 12288 MiB (MemAvailable 9674 MiB; cgroup v2: limit 24576
  MiB, working set ... MiB, room ... MiB; ZFS ARC 14336 MiB, its floor 1024
  MiB (not counted in MemAvailable))

Specs: the reading keeps each cgroup's figures, parses the ARC and decides
nothing on it, and gives the same figure as before; a refusal names the
reading it was decided on, read once (it fails without this change).

Assisted-by: Claude Code
